==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=hotbed}}

===Etymology===
{{compound|en|hot|bed}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A low bed of [[earth]] covered with [[glass]], and heated with [[rotting]] [[manure]], used for the [[germination]] of [[seed]]s and the [[growth]] of [[tender]] [[plant]]s, like a miniature [[hothouse]].
#* {{quote-book|en |year=1924|title=Control of the Codling Moth in the Pacific Northwest |author=Erval Jackson Newcomer, M. A. Yothers, Warren Draper Whitcomb |page=11|ISBN=|passage=Tomato seedlings will be ready to transplant from the '''hotbed''' to the coldframe in 12 to 18 days after sowing the seed, depending upon the amount of sunshine and the care given the bed. }}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} An [[environment]] that is [[ideal]] for the growth or [[development]] of something, especially of something [[undesirable]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 August 12|author=Nigel Harris|title=Comment: Rail at the brink? Not yet...|journal=Rail|page=3|text=The problem faced by Government and railway alike is the dreadful legacy of the 'stay home' policy which has left millions of people fearing that trains are a '''hotbed''' of infection to be avoided like the... well, do I have to say it?!}}
#: {{syn|en|seedbed}}
# An iron platform in a rolling mill, on which hot bars, rails, etc., are laid to cool.

